(a) The public officer may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building, or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial, or business use if he/she finds that conditions exist in such building, dwelling, or structure which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety, or morals of the occupants of such dwelling, building, or structure; of the occupants of neighborhood dwelling, buildings, or structures; or of other residents of the city. Such conditions include the following (without limiting the generality of the foregoing):
(1) Those buildings, dwellings or structures whose interior or exterior bearing walls, or other vertical structural members list, lean or buckle to such extent as to weaken the structural support of such member or members below the minimum required by the building codes of the city;
(2) Those buildings, dwellings or structures, which have improperly distributed loads upon the floors or roofs or in which the same are overloaded, or which have insufficient strength to be reasonably safe for the purpose for which it is used, or intended to be used;
(3) Those buildings, dwellings or structures, which have visible soil erosion adjacent to or under any structural support;
(4) Those buildings, dwellings or structures, which have parts that are so attached or detached that they are likely to fall and injure persons or property;
(5) Those buildings, dwellings or structures, which are unfit for human habitation or for commercial, industrial or business uses due to dilapidation and defects increasing the hazards of fire, accident or other calamities;
(6) Those buildings, dwellings or structures having inadequate facilities for egress in the event of a catastrophe, such as windstorm, fire, earthquake or panic because of inadequate stairways, elevators, escalators or fire escapes, such that such defect is likely to cause injury to persons or property in the event of such a catastrophe;
(7) Those buildings, dwellings or structures which have been damaged by fire, wind or other causes so as to have become dangerous to life, safety or the general health and welfare of the occupants thereof or the people of the city;
(8) Those buildings, dwellings or structures which have become so dilapidated, decayed, damaged, unsafe, unsanitary, or which so utterly fail to provide the amenities essential to decent living, including without limitation, effective and adequate ventilation, plumbing, sanitation facilities, running water, light or other facilities such that such conditions render the dwellings, buildings, or structure dangerous or detrimental to the health, safety, morals, or welfare of either those living therein, those who may live therein, or the people of the city;
(9) Those buildings, dwellings or structures where an accumulation of weeds, trash, junk, filth and other unsanitary or unsafe conditions shall create a public health hazard or general nuisance to those persons residing in the vicinity;
(10) Those buildings, dwellings or structures which exist in violation of any ordinances of the city such that said building, dwelling or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial business or industrial use due to conditions existing in such building, dwelling or structure which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety or morals of the occupants of such building, dwelling or structure, of the occupants of the neighborhood buildings, dwellings or structures, or of other residents of the city;
(11) Those buildings, dwellings, or structures that have been used:
a. On more than two occasions within a six-month period as the site of the unlawful sale, delivery, manufacture, or cultivation of any controlled substance;
b. On one occasion as the site of the unlawful possession of a controlled substance, where such possession constitutes a felony and that has been previously used on more than one occasion as the site of the unlawful sale, delivery, manufacture, or cultivation of any controlled substance;
c. By a criminal gang for the purpose of conducting a pattern of criminal gang activity;
d. On more than two occasions within a six-month period, as the site of a violation relating to dealing in stolen property;
e. On more than two occasions within a six-month period, as the site of a violation relating to prostitution;
(12) Those conditions constituting nuisances set forth in section 14-51 of this Code;
(13) Any building, structure, or other place or location where any activity is conducted, performed, or maintained in violation of local, state, or federal law (which can be a safety hazard);
(14) Abandoned vehicles. It shall be unlawful to keep or maintain more than two abandoned vehicles as defined by this ordinance, and any abandoned vehicle is hereby declared to be a public nuisance and shall be abated as provided in this ordinance. Any junked automobile, truck, vehicles or trailer of any kind or type shall not be parked or stand on any private property or public roads and is hereby found to create a condition tending to reduce the value of private property, to promote blight deterioration, and invite plundering and vandalism, to create fire hazards, to constitute a nuisance creating a hazard to the health and safety to Rome's existing Unsafe Building Ordinance; or
(15) Other additional standards which may from time to time be adopted and referenced herein by ordinance amendment.
(b) The public officer may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building, or structure is vacant or dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of a nuisance activity based upon personal observation or report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of a nuisance activity being committed.
(1) A nuisance activity includes:
a. A most serious offense.
b. A drug related activity.
c. Any of the following activities, behaviors, or criminal conduct:
1. Assault, fighting, menacing, stalking, harassment or reckless endangerment.
2. Promoting, advancing, or profiting from prostitution.
3. Prostitution.
4. Permitting prostitution.
5. Obstructing pedestrian or vehicular traffic.
6. Failure to disperse.
7. Weapons violations.
8. Drug traffic loitering.
9. Gang related activity.
(Ord. No. 04-7-2, § I, 7-19-04; Ord. No. 2018-8-3, § I, 8-13-18)

